1.text:
The two inbred parental strains have haplotypes that may be denoted as \(AAAAA\) and \(BBBBB\) at the five SNP loci. All gametes produced by the F1 therefore experience exactly one crossover, which can occur in any of the four intervals lying between the five loci. This produces exactly eight possible haplotypes in the F2 generation:

- starting with the A haplotype and switching once: \(ABBBB\), \(AABBB\), \(AAABB\), \(AAAAB\);
- starting with the B haplotype and switching once: \(BAAAA\), \(BBAAA\), \(BBBAA\), \(BBBBA\).

Every F2 individual therefore carries a pair of these single-switch haplotypes. Gametes that give rise to the F3 generation again experience exactly one crossover, now between the two single-switch chromosomes carried by the F2 parent. The resulting F3 haplotypes are consequently assembled from at most three contiguous segments drawn from the original parental strains and therefore contain at most two switches.

The complete set of binary strings of length 5 that contain at most two switches is obtained by direct enumeration:

- zero switches: \(AAAAA\), \(BBBBB\) (2 strings);
- exactly one switch: 4 possible intervals \(\times\) 2 starting alleles = 8 strings;
- exactly two switches: \(\binom{4}{2}\) pairs of intervals \(\times\) 2 starting alleles = 12 strings.

Hence exactly \(2+8+12=22\) distinct haplotypes are possible. No haplotype containing three or more switches can be generated under the stated constraint of precisely one crossover per meiosis.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

